1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,763
Phagocytosis and the respiratory burst.

2
00:00:02,813 --> 00:00:08,243
A neutrophil must leave the blood, find the microbe, swallow it and kill it.

3
00:00:08,293 --> 00:00:13,580
Exam questions test each step, and the disease that appears when it fails.

4
00:00:13,933 --> 00:00:15,920
Neutrophils come first.

5
00:00:15,970 --> 00:00:21,677
An adult makes more than one hundred billion neutrophils per day.

6
00:00:21,727 --> 00:00:25,574
G-CSF and GM-CSF stimulate their production.

7
00:00:25,933 --> 00:00:28,240
Step one is to leave the blood.

8
00:00:28,290 --> 00:00:32,954
Abbas describes four steps: selectin rolling, chemokine-driven

9
00:00:33,004 --> 00:00:37,667
integrin activation, firm integrin arrest, and transmigration.

10
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:40,960
Rolling starts in postcapillary venules.

11
00:00:41,010 --> 00:00:44,606
Selectins mediate a first, low-affinity adhesion.

12
00:00:44,656 --> 00:00:48,475
Endothelial cells express P-selectin and E-selectin.

13
00:00:48,525 --> 00:00:52,716
L-selectin sits on the leukocyte, not on the endothelium.

14
00:00:53,067 --> 00:00:56,704
P-selectin is stored in endothelial granules.

15
00:00:56,754 --> 00:01:01,130
Histamine and thrombin move it rapidly to the surface.

16
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:05,637
IL-1 and TNF induce E-selectin within one to two hours.

17
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,505
The leukocyte ligand is the sugar sialyl Lewis X.

18
00:01:09,555 --> 00:01:13,349
Selectin bonds have low affinity and a fast off-rate.

19
00:01:13,399 --> 00:01:17,339
They form and break again and again, so the cell rolls.

20
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:19,451
Next, integrin activation.

21
00:01:19,501 --> 00:01:24,992
Endothelial heparan sulfate holds chemokines and shows them to the rolling cell.

22
00:01:25,042 --> 00:01:29,425
Chemokine receptor signals switch the integrin to high affinity.

23
00:01:29,475 --> 00:01:31,988
This is called inside-out signalling.

24
00:01:32,367 --> 00:01:33,877
Then firm arrest.

25
00:01:33,927 --> 00:01:39,016
Cytokines increase ICAM-1 and VCAM-1 on the endothelium.

26
00:01:39,066 --> 00:01:41,677
ICAM-1 binds LFA-1 and MAC-1.

27
00:01:41,727 --> 00:01:43,421
VCAM-1 binds VLA-4.

28
00:01:43,471 --> 00:01:44,798
The cell stops.

29
00:01:45,167 --> 00:01:46,778
Last, transmigration.

30
00:01:46,828 --> 00:01:51,920
The cell squeezes between endothelial cells, with help from CD31.

31
00:01:51,970 --> 00:01:53,977
Watch the word diapedesis.

32
00:01:54,027 --> 00:01:56,430
Abbas uses it for this passage.

33
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:00,465
Janeway uses it for crossing the basement membrane.

34
00:02:00,833 --> 00:02:03,120
Now the cell must find the microbe.

35
00:02:03,170 --> 00:02:08,062
Chemotaxis is movement along a chemoattractant gradient toward its source.

36
00:02:08,112 --> 00:02:12,869
Chemokine receptors are seven-transmembrane G protein-coupled receptors.

37
00:02:13,233 --> 00:02:21,136
Neutrophils use CXCR1 and CXCR2 to bind CXCL8, also called IL-8.

38
00:02:21,186 --> 00:02:28,218
Classical monocytes use CCR2, and its key ligand is CCL2.

39
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:33,291
Other attractants are C5a, C3a and leukotriene B4.

40
00:02:33,341 --> 00:02:41,257
Bacterial formyl peptides bind FPR1, and they are among the most potent attractants.

41
00:02:41,307 --> 00:02:46,093
C5a and CXCL8 also switch on the respiratory burst.

42
00:02:46,467 --> 00:02:47,815
Step three: swallow.

43
00:02:47,865 --> 00:02:51,591
Phagocytosis is an active, energy-dependent engulfment

44
00:02:51,641 --> 00:02:54,598
of particles larger than half a micrometre.

45
00:02:54,648 --> 00:02:55,927
Opsonization helps.

46
00:02:55,977 --> 00:03:01,591
Antibodies or complement coat the microbe, so phagocytes take it up more readily.

47
00:03:01,967 --> 00:03:05,318
Each opsonin has its receptor.

48
00:03:05,368 --> 00:03:11,213
FcγRI is a high-affinity receptor for IgG1 and IgG3.

49
00:03:11,263 --> 00:03:13,708
CR1 binds C3b and C4b.

50
00:03:13,758 --> 00:03:20,964
CR1 alone is weak, but IgG binding or interferon gamma helps it.

51
00:03:21,333 --> 00:03:28,653
CR3 binds iC3b, and this binding alone is enough to stimulate phagocytosis.

52
00:03:28,703 --> 00:03:34,254
CR3 is the same molecule as MAC-1, the adhesion integrin.

53
00:03:34,304 --> 00:03:36,514
Its beta chain is CD18.

54
00:03:36,564 --> 00:03:38,872
Remember this for LAD-1.

55
00:03:39,233 --> 00:03:40,484
The steps are simple.

56
00:03:40,534 --> 00:03:42,280
The microbe binds a receptor.

57
00:03:42,330 --> 00:03:45,625
The membrane forms a cup that closes into a phagosome.

58
00:03:45,675 --> 00:03:49,342
The phagosome fuses with a lysosome to make a phagolysosome.

59
00:03:49,392 --> 00:03:51,014
Most killing happens there.

60
00:03:51,367 --> 00:03:52,577
Step four: kill.

61
00:03:52,627 --> 00:03:57,067
The three most important classes of killing molecules are

62
00:03:57,117 --> 00:04:01,952
reactive oxygen species, nitric oxide and proteolytic enzymes.

63
00:04:02,333 --> 00:04:10,585
Reactive oxygen species come from phagocyte oxidase, also called NADPH oxidase.

64
00:04:10,635 --> 00:04:17,101
In resting neutrophils, gp91 and p22 sit in granule membranes.

65
00:04:17,151 --> 00:04:21,094
p40, p47 and p67 float in the cytosol.

66
00:04:21,144 --> 00:04:23,931
Rac2 signals assemble them.

67
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:25,455
Now the chain.

68
00:04:25,505 --> 00:04:29,156
NADPH oxidase reduces oxygen to superoxide.

69
00:04:29,206 --> 00:04:34,493
Superoxide dismutase converts superoxide to hydrogen peroxide.

70
00:04:34,543 --> 00:04:38,280
Myeloperoxidase turns hydrogen peroxide into

71
00:04:38,330 --> 00:04:42,067
hypohalous acids, such as hypochlorous acid.

72
00:04:42,433 --> 00:04:46,604
It is called the respiratory burst because it consumes oxygen.

73
00:04:46,654 --> 00:04:50,485
Potassium and hydrogen ions then enter the phagolysosome.

74
00:04:50,535 --> 00:04:55,727
This releases and activates granule enzymes such as elastase and cathepsin G.

75
00:04:55,777 --> 00:04:59,403
Janeway notes that oxygen radicals alone may not kill.

76
00:04:59,767 --> 00:05:02,355
Nitric oxide is the second weapon.

77
00:05:02,405 --> 00:05:07,399
Inducible nitric oxide synthase is absent in resting macrophages.

78
00:05:07,449 --> 00:05:11,744
TLR signals induce it, especially with interferon gamma.

79
00:05:11,794 --> 00:05:17,409
Nitric oxide joins superoxide or hydrogen peroxide to make peroxynitrite.

80
00:05:17,459 --> 00:05:20,746
Neutrophils make little or no nitric oxide.

81
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:26,372
Neutrophils can also throw out NETs, neutrophil extracellular traps.

82
00:05:26,422 --> 00:05:30,990
They are strands of DNA and histones with granule proteins.

83
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:33,965
NETs trap and kill bacteria and fungi.

84
00:05:34,015 --> 00:05:39,913
Their formation needs PAD4, elastase, myeloperoxidase and phagocyte oxidase.

85
00:05:39,963 --> 00:05:42,105
The neutrophil usually dies.

86
00:05:42,467 --> 00:05:43,522
Now the clinic.

87
00:05:43,572 --> 00:05:47,429
Phagocyte defects cause invasive bacterial and fungal

88
00:05:47,479 --> 00:05:50,966
infections of skin, liver and respiratory tract.

89
00:05:51,016 --> 00:05:54,283
The fungi are mainly Aspergillus and Candida.

90
00:05:54,333 --> 00:06:00,327
Janeway sorts them into four groups: production, adhesion, activation and killing.

91
00:06:00,700 --> 00:06:06,186
Chronic granulomatous disease, or CGD, is the classic killing defect.

92
00:06:06,236 --> 00:06:11,803
Mutations hit NADPH oxidase, so phagocytes make too little superoxide.

93
00:06:11,853 --> 00:06:15,654
About two-thirds of cases are X-linked, in gp91.

94
00:06:15,704 --> 00:06:19,746
The rest are autosomal recessive and can be milder.

95
00:06:20,100 --> 00:06:25,456
Catalase-producing organisms, such as Staphylococcus, are a problem in CGD.

96
00:06:25,506 --> 00:06:29,637
Their catalase destroys the little hydrogen peroxide left.

97
00:06:29,687 --> 00:06:34,035
Invasive Aspergillus infection is the leading cause of death.

98
00:06:34,085 --> 00:06:37,639
Uncontrolled infection drives granuloma formation.

99
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,611
Abbas lists management as antibiotic and antifungal prophylaxis,

100
00:06:42,661 --> 00:06:46,761
interferon gamma, and possible stem cell transplantation.

101
00:06:46,811 --> 00:06:48,800
Check the current guideline.

102
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:54,262
Myeloperoxidase deficiency also impairs killing, but it is milder than CGD.

103
00:06:54,633 --> 00:06:59,205
Leukocyte adhesion deficiencies are autosomal recessive.

104
00:06:59,255 --> 00:07:03,826
Neutrophils cannot reach the tissue, so there is no pus.

105
00:07:03,876 --> 00:07:08,448
LAD-1 is a CD18 defect, so beta-2 integrins are missing.

106
00:07:08,498 --> 00:07:11,336
The blood often shows leukocytosis.

107
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:13,712
LAD-2 is a rolling defect.

108
00:07:13,762 --> 00:07:18,470
A GDP-fucose transporter fails, so sialyl Lewis X is absent.

109
00:07:18,520 --> 00:07:23,149
Look for developmental problems and the Bombay blood group.

110
00:07:23,199 --> 00:07:25,369
LAD-3 is a KINDLIN-3 defect.

111
00:07:25,419 --> 00:07:31,555
Integrins cannot be activated, and patients bleed with normal platelet counts.

112
00:07:31,933 --> 00:07:35,866
Chédiak-Higashi syndrome is autosomal recessive.

113
00:07:35,916 --> 00:07:39,765
LYST mutations disturb lysosome trafficking, so

114
00:07:39,815 --> 00:07:43,664
phagosomes and lysosomes fail to fuse properly.

115
00:07:43,714 --> 00:07:49,223
Look for giant lysosomes, partial albinism and pyogenic infections.

116
00:07:49,273 --> 00:07:52,624
Without transplant, HLH usually develops.

117
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,266
Production defects come last.

118
00:07:55,316 --> 00:07:58,940
Severe congenital neutropenia has a neutrophil

119
00:07:58,990 --> 00:08:02,774
count below 0.5 times ten to the nine per litre.

120
00:08:02,824 --> 00:08:06,448
Cyclic neutropenia cycles about every 21 days.

121
00:08:06,498 --> 00:08:12,359
ELA2 mutations are the most common cause of severe congenital neutropenia.

122
00:08:12,409 --> 00:08:15,154
G-CSF is the mainstay of treatment.

123
00:08:15,533 --> 00:08:17,957
Five things to remember.

124
00:08:18,007 --> 00:08:22,285
One: roll, activate, arrest, transmigrate.

125
00:08:22,335 --> 00:08:28,056
Two: CXCL8, C5a and formyl peptides attract neutrophils.

126
00:08:28,106 --> 00:08:31,662
Three: CR3 is MAC-1 and binds iC3b.

127
00:08:31,712 --> 00:08:35,991
Four: oxidase, dismutase, myeloperoxidase.

128
00:08:36,041 --> 00:08:41,658
Five: CGD, LAD and Chédiak-Higashi each block one step.

129
00:08:42,033 --> 00:08:43,203
One exam trap.

130
00:08:43,253 --> 00:08:48,606
Myeloperoxidase does not make superoxide or hydrogen peroxide.

131
00:08:48,656 --> 00:08:55,664
NADPH oxidase makes superoxide, and superoxide dismutase makes hydrogen peroxide.

132
00:08:55,714 --> 00:09:00,020
So a defect in myeloperoxidase is milder than CGD.

133
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:02,521
Next: complement made easy.

134
00:09:02,571 --> 00:09:07,827
Three pathways, one C3 convertase, and the opsonins you met today.
